The '''Astrocystitidae''' are a small, extinct family of [[edrioblastoid]] [[edrioasteroids]] that are generally rare in the fossil record.  They are characterized by possession of a stalk and theca shape convergent with the [[Blastoidea]].

==Classification==

===History===

''Astrocystites ottawaensis'' was initially classified as a [[cystoid]], a group that at that time included all stalked echinoderms that were neither [[crinoids]] nor [[blastoids]].  It was subsequently placed by different authors with either the blastoids or (somewhat more frequently) the edrioasteroids, although a resemblence to the [[parablastoids]] was also noted.<ref>{{harvnb|Fay|1967|pp=S292</ref> Despite the trend towards considering ''Astrocystites'' to be an edrioasteroid, the class Edrioblastoidea was erected by Robert O. Fay to contain the family in 1962.<ref>{{harvnb|Fay|1967|pp=S290}}</ref>

===Equivalence with edrioblastoids ''sensu stricto''===

Traditionally, the Astrocystitidae have been the only edrioblastoid family, including after the class's demotion to the edrioasteroid order Edrioblastoida in 1990 by Smith and Jell.<ref>{{harvnb|Smith|Jell|1990|p=742}}</ref>

More recently, edrioblastoids were further demoted by Guensburg and Sprinkle to the suborder Edrioblastoidina within the order Edrioasterida, and expanded to include the cyathocystids and rhenopyrgids as either one<ref>{{harvnb|Guensburg|Sprinkle|1994|pp=20–21}}</ref> or two<ref>{{harvnb|Sumrall|Herida|Rodríguez|Mestre|2013|p=773}}</ref> additional families.  A possible relationship among these groups had been noted at the time the order Edrioblastoida was established.<ref>{{harvnb|Smith|Jell|1990|pp=744–745, 750}}</ref>

Even among authors that use the expanded suborder, the term "edrioblastoid" is often used informally to refer only to astrocystitids, as opposed to cyathocystids and/or rhenopyrgids.<ref>{{harvnb|Sprinkle|Sumrall|p=346}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Sumrall|Herida|Rodríguez|Mestre|2013|p=766}}</ref>

