Between its definition in 1897 and the discovery of ``Astrocystites distans`` (Webby) in 1968, the Astrocystitidae consisted only of ``Astrocystites ottawaensis``.  It's placement was the subject of much debate, and it was eventually placed in its own class, Edrioblastoidea, by Fay in 1962.  This was accepted by the [[Treatise on Invertebrate Paleontology]] in 1967, but was already being called into quetion by Webby in 1968, in favor of placement within the Edrioasteroidea.

The latter position eventually won out, and in 1990 Smith and Jell demoted the class Edrioblastoidea to the order Edrioblastoida, still consisting only of the Astrocystitidae, to which the genus ``Cambroblastus`` was also added.  In 1994, Guensburg and Sprinkle further demoted the group to the suborder Edrioblastoidina, which contained the cyathocystids and rhenopyrgids in addition to Astrocystitidae.  Both arrangments have been cited in more recent papers.

